          ],
          "content": "Introduction to Linear Polynomials"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "23"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.108,
            0.883,
            0.149
          ],
          "content": "Observe that the amount left on the $n ^ { t h }$ day will be \\`(100 – 5n). Therefore, on the 12th day the amount left will be $(100 - 12 \\times 5) = 540$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.164,
            0.399,
            0.188
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.201,
            0.867,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "What amount will be left on the 15th day? How many days will it take for the entire amount to be spent?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.258,
            0.885,
            0.315
          ],
          "content": "**Example 8:** An auto-rikshaw fare starts at \\`25 and remains the same for the initial 2 km. Then it increases by \\`15 per km. What will be the fare for a travel of 10 km?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.315,
            0.885,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": "For the initial 2 km, the fare is \\`25. For every kilometre (km) thereafter, the fare will increase by \\`15. Therefore, the total fare for a travel of 10 km will be $25+15\\times8=7145$"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.382,
            0.88,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": "| Km travelled | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Fare (`) | 25 | 25 | 25 + 1 × 15= 40 | 25 + 2 × 15= 55 | 25 + 3 × 15= 70 | 25 + 4 × 15= 85 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_table_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.466,
            0.885,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": "Observe that the total fare for a travel of n km will be $25+15\\times(n-2)=15n-5$ when $n \\geq 2$ . Here, the fare for a specific distance covered is a function of the distance n km."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.55,
            0.399,
            0.574
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.587,
            0.531,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "For how many km will the fare be \\`130?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.639,
            0.886,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": "Note that in all the above examples, the $n ^ { t h }$ term is a linear expression in n. A **linear pattern** is a sequence of numbers where the difference between two consecutive terms is constant. We will learn more about linear patterns in the chapter on Sequences and Progressions."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.742,
            0.622,
            0.771
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 2.3"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.79,
            0.334,
            0.811
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Solve the following:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.814,
            0.886,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "1. A student has \\`500 in her savings bank account. She gets \\`150 every month as pocket money. How much money will she have at the end of every month from the second month onwards? Find a linear expression to represent the amount she will have in the $n ^ { t h }$ month."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
